 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Resistive load dissipation",
   "expr": "PL = IL² × RO",
   "where": {
    "PL": "driver dissipation from a continuous load current",
    "IL": "load current",
    "RO": "driver output resistance"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 9
  },
  {
   "name": "Capacitive load dissipation",
   "expr": "PL = f × CL × VS²",
   "where": {
    "f": "switching frequency",
    "CL": "load capacitance",
    "VS": "supply voltage"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 9
  },
  {
   "name": "Transition (crossover) dissipation",
   "expr": "PT = 2 × f × VS × Q",
   "where": {
    "f": "switching frequency",
    "VS": "supply voltage",
    "Q": "charge lost per transition, given against supply voltage by the crossover energy loss curve"
   },
   "note": "Total dissipation in practice is PL + PT; quiescent dissipation is negligible.",
   "page": 9
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Configurations",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Three versions share one pinout: MIC4426 has two inverting drivers, MIC4427 two non-inverting drivers, and MIC4428 an inverting driver on channel A with a non-inverting driver on channel B. They are pin-compatible upgrades for the MIC426/427/428 and MIC1426/1427/1428. The MIC4126/4127/4128 family is pointed to for higher power and narrow-pulse work."
  },
  {
   "topic": "What it drives",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Built on a BiCMOS/DMOS process, each channel turns a TTL or CMOS logic level into an output that comes within 25 mV of VS or ground. Power MOSFET gates are the main load; capacitive, resistive and inductive loads needing a low-impedance, fast, high-peak-current drive also suit it, such as heavily loaded clock lines, coaxial cable and piezoelectric transducers. The limit on any load is the package's power dissipation. Headline figures: 1.5 A peak output, 1000 pF switched in 25 ns, matched rise and fall times, about 7 Ω output impedance, under 40 ns typical delay, about 6 pF equivalent input capacitance, supply current 4 mA with inputs high and 400 µA with inputs low."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Ruggedness",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Each part survives reverse current of either polarity up to 500 mA without latching up, and ground-pin noise spikes of up to 5 V of either polarity. Inputs are protected down to -5 V, and the parts have ESD protection."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply bypassing",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Fast edges into large capacitive loads draw large supply currents: moving 1000 pF through 16 V in 25 ns takes 0.8 A. Keep the supply low-impedance over a wide band with capacitors in parallel, ceramic MLC parts of low inductance on leads shorter than 0.5 in. Usually 1.0 µF of film in parallel with 0.1 µF ceramic MLC parts (one or two of them) is enough."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Grounding",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "With the inverting channels (MIC4426, MIC4428), give the input circuit and the output circuit separate ground returns, or use a ground plane, for best switching speed. Voltage dropped between the driver's ground and the input signal's ground during high-current switching acts as negative feedback and slows the edges."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Control inputs",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "An unused input must go to a logic high (VS will do) or to ground; grounding unused inputs gives the lowest quiescent current, under 500 µA, while a high input can make the driver draw up to 9 mA. The inputs have 100 mV of hysteresis, which keeps transitions clean and limits current spikes in the output stage. The threshold is about 1.5 V and does not depend on supply voltage; anything from 1.5 V up to VS reads as high, with under 1 µA of input current over that range. The drivers can be run from switch-mode controllers such as the TL494, SG1526/7, MIC38C42 and TSC170."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power dissipation",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Check the dissipation against the thermal ratings. Quiescent dissipation can be ignored; the working total is the load dissipation plus the transition dissipation (PL + PT). Load dissipation comes from a resistive load current through RO, or from charging a capacitive load each cycle. Transition dissipation appears at high switching frequency, when both output FETs, P-channel and N-channel, conduct together for a moment at each edge; its charge per transition Q rises with supply voltage, from about 1.3 nC near 5 V to about 9.6 nC near 17 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Package marking",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "SOIC and PDIP parts carry three lines: MIC, then the device and suffix code (for example 4426YM or 4427ZN), then a week code and traceability code. MSOP parts carry the device number on the first line and the suffix (for example YMM) on the second. The -TR tape-and-reel suffix is for ordering only and is not marked on the part. Packages are Pb-free; pin 1 is shown by a dot or a triangle."
  }
 ],
